<article class="env-lemma" id="145B5B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/145B5B">Lemma <span data-tag="145B5B">3.1.3.2</span>.</a> Let $f: \calC \rightarrow \calD $ be a functor between $\infty $-categories. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The functor $f$ is a categorical equivalence. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
For every simplicial set $K$, the induced map $\Fun (K,\calC ) \rightarrow \Fun (K,\calD )$ is a categorical equivalence. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
For every simplicial set $K$, the functor $\Fun (K,\calC ) \rightarrow \Fun (K,\calD )$ induces a homotopy equivalence between the largest Kan complex contained in $\Fun (K,\calC )$ and the largest Kan complex contained in $\Fun (K,\calD )$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implications $(1) \Rightarrow (2) \Rightarrow (3)$ are obvious. Suppose that $(3)$ is satisfied. Let $K = \calD $. According to $(3)$, there exists an object $x$ of $\Fun (K,\calC )$ whose image in $\Fun (K,\calD )$ is equivalent to the identity map $K \rightarrow \calD $. We may identify $x$ with a functor $g: \calD \rightarrow \calC $ having the property that $f \circ g$ is homotopic to the identity $\id _{\calD }$. It follows that $g$ also has the property asserted by $(3)$, so the same argument shows that there is a functor $f': \calC \rightarrow \calD $ such that $g \circ f'$ is homotopic to $\id _{\calC }$. It follows that $f \circ g \circ f'$ is homotopic to both $f$ and $f'$, so that $f$ is homotopic to $f'$. Thus $g$ is a homotopy inverse to $f$, which proves that $f$ is an equivalence. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>